🏆 SYSTEM DESIGN: URL SHORTENER (TINYURL / BITLY)

FAANG-ready. College-placement optimized. Complete.

⭐ 1) Requirements (Functional + Non-functional) — 1 minute
✅ Functional Requirements:

Given a long URL → generate a short URL.

Given a short URL → redirect to original URL.

Ensure short URL uniqueness.

Support high read traffic (redirects).

🎯 Non-functional Requirements:

Low latency redirect (target: < 5 ms).

High availability (service must always work).

Scalable (billions of URLs).

Clarifying Questions:

Allowed characters? → Assume alphanumeric (62 chars).

Same long URL submitted twice → new short URL? → Assume YES.

Expiry needed? → Optional.

⭐ 2) API Design — 30 seconds
POST /shorten

Body: { "longUrl": "https://abc.com/xyz" }
Response: { "shortUrl": "https://tiny.url/Af91bX" }

GET /{shortId}

Redirects to long URL.

That’s enough.

⭐ 3) Estimation (Quick & Useful) — 2 minutes

Assume:

Writes: 1M URLs/day ≈ 12 writes/sec

Reads: 10x reads → 120 reads/sec

Avg long URL size: ~500 bytes

Storage per day:

1M × 500 bytes = ~0.5 GB/day
≈ 180 GB/year


Conclusion:

Must use distributed storage.

Must use caching (since reads >> writes).

⭐ 4) High-Level Architecture — 2 minutes
Client → Load Balancer → App Servers → Cache (Redis) → Database (NoSQL)

Components:

Load Balancer → Distribute traffic

App Servers → Stateless, easy to scale

Redis Cache → Fast redirects

Database (NoSQL like Cassandra/DynamoDB)

Key-value: shortId → longUrl

Why NoSQL?

Extremely fast key-value lookups

Horizontal scalability

Handles billions of rows

⭐ 5) Data Model (Simple & Perfect) — 1 minute
Table: URLMapping
Field	Type	Notes
shortId	string (PK)	Unique, 7 chars
longUrl	string	Original URL
createdAt	timestamp	Metadata
expiry	timestamp (optional)	TTL

Primary Key → shortId (perfect for KV lookup).

⭐ 6) LLD (Low-Level Design, 1 minute)

Lightweight LLD — enough for placements

Classes:
1. URLMapping

shortId

longUrl

createdAt

expiry

2. URLShortenerService

generateShortId()

createMapping(longUrl)

resolve(shortId)

3. URLRepository

save(shortId, longUrl)

fetch(shortId)

Relationship:
Controller → Service → Repository → DB
                   ↘ Cache ↙


Keeps responsibilities clean (SRP).

⭐ 7) Deep Dive: Short ID Generation (Most Important Part) — 2 minutes
🎯 Approach Used: Auto-increment ID → Base62 encoding

This is the best and most interview-friendly approach.

Steps:

Keep a counter: 1, 2, 3, 4, …

Convert this number to Base62 (0–9, a–z, A–Z)

Base62 makes ID short & compact

Example:

125 → “cb”
5000000 → “4c92”

Why This Is Best?

Guaranteed unique

Very fast

No collisions

Easy to shard ID-generation

⭐ 8) Caching Layer (Very Important Concept) — 1 minute

Redirects are read-heavy, so Redis cache is critical.

Cache Flow:

User clicks short URL

App checks Redis:
✔ If found → return 301 redirect (fast)
✖ If not → fetch from DB → store in Redis → return result

Cache Strategy:

Cache Aside Pattern (industry standard)

LRU eviction or TTL-based eviction

⭐ 9) Scaling + Sharding + Replication — 2 minutes
✔ 1. Sharding

We can shard DB by:

shardId = hash(shortId) % N


Ensures even distribution.

✔ 2. Replication

1 leader + 2 followers per shard

Ensures high availability

✔ 3. App Server Scaling

Stateless servers

Can auto-scale horizontally

✔ 4. CDN (Optional)

Used only if we serve static preview pages.

⭐ 10) Failures + Bottlenecks (Critical for scoring) — 1 minute
Redis down?

Fallback to database

Slight latency increase

DB primary down?

Failover to replica

High write spikes?

Buffer via queue (Kafka/SQS)

Smooth ingestion

Hot shortId shard?

Re-sharding

Consistent hashing

Mention 2–3 only.

